[[Image:Breads.jpg|thumb|Zwei '''Brote''' — Two loaves of '''bread''' (1, 2)]]
[[Image:Rye bread.JPG|thumb|Mehrere Scheiben Roggen'''brot''' — Several slices of rye '''bread''' (3)]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{m|gmh|brōt}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|brōt}} (attested since the 8th century), from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*braudą}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰrew-||to seethe, to boil}}; an extension of {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰerə-||to well up, to boil, to be in motion (as of fire or water)}}. Originating from the same root are {{term|Brühe|lang=de}}, {{term|Brei|lang=de}}, {{term|braten|lang=de}} and {{term|brennen|lang=de}}.

''Germanic Cognates'' with identical meaning include Old Saxon {{m|osx|brōd}} (whence Low German {{term|Broot|lang=nds}}, {{term|Brot|lang=nds}}), Old Dutch {{m|odt|*brōd}} (whence Dutch {{term|brood|lang=nl}}, Afrikaans {{term|brood|lang=af}}), Old English {{m|ang|brēad}} (whence Modern English {{term|bread|lang=en}}, Scots {{term|breid|lang=sco}}), West Frisian {{term|brea|lang=fy}} and Old Norse {{term|brauð|lang=non}} (whence Icelandic {{term|brauð|lang=is}}, Faroese {{term|breyð|lang=fo}}, Norwegian {{term|brød|lang=no}}, Swedish {{term|bröd|lang=sv}}, Danish {{term|brød|lang=da}}).

''Non-Germanic-Cognates'' include Ancient Greek {{term|sc=polytonic|βρύτος||fermented barley beverage|lang=grc}}, Latin {{term|ferveo||to be hot, to boil, to burn|lang=la}}, {{term|fermentum||fermentation, leaven|lang=la}}, Albanian {{term|mbruaj||to knead|lang=sq}}, Old Armenian {{term|բորբ|sc=Armn||bright, aflame|lang=xcl}}. Originally, the meaning of ''Brot'' was ''"what has been fermented, leaven"'' and may be a nominal derivative from {{etyl|gem-pro|-}} {{m|gem-pro|*brewwaną||to brew}} (whence German {{term|brauen|lang=de}}). It replaced the older {{term|Laib||loaf|lang=de}} which was the more common term in Old High German (compare the use of {{m|ang|hlāf}} and {{m|ang|brēad}} in Old English).<ref>Pfeifer, Wolfgang. 1995, 2005. ''Etymologisches Wörterbuch des Deutschen.'' München: dtv. ISBN 3423325119.</ref>

Alternative etymologies derive ''Brot'' and its cognates from {{etyl|gem-pro|-}} {{m|gem-pro|*braudaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*brauþaz||broken piece, fragment}} and from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰera-||to split, beat, hew, struggle}} (compare Swedish {{term|bryta||to break|lang=sv}}, Albanian {{term|brydh||weak, mild|lang=sq}}, English {{term|brittle|lang=sv}}). A conflation of both roots could also be possible.

